an of  that for them  and i'm a fan of allowing people to  pursue what their preferences  are i really think it's nice i like what  our friend  um uh graham hill is doing with the  tiny apartments and uh especially in  cities like new york city  where we he's in our documentary and  they have that 420 square foot apartment  to sleep seven and you can have dinner  for  10 in in the house and and you can have  a movie theater screening of like with  eight people and  all in this 400 square foot place  because it's modular and things come out  of the ceiling and the floor and you've  got a stand-up desk and a cat  you have all of these things where  they're using that space to the best  of their ability and i really like that  because that 400 square feet feels  way bigger because they have some taller  ceilings  it feels way bigger than 400 square feet  now  becks and ella and i live in a place  that is probably 900 square feet  i don't know i don't measure my square  footage but what i know  is it's appropriately sized for us there  are two bedrooms there's a living room  slash dining slash kitchen area  and a couple bathrooms and that's really  it that's what is appropriate  for us however i that wasn't always  appropriate for me i used to live in  a loft apartment because that is what  worked for me when i was  single it was just me by myself let's  just have this this one room  that is appropriate for me so what is  appropriate today  may change tomorrow the living space  that you need right now  might not be the living space that you  need tomorrow i think about the empty  nesters who keep  their you know big five bedroom houses  and all four kids are out of the house  and  all of a sudden they're like well i  guess we'll just hang around here  we've got way too much house and so what  do they try to do they try to cram a  life  into that house because it's all they  know  as opposed to finding a new house that  fits whatever their new lifestyle  is and so regard